{
  "_comment": "Round-15: a red team was told to defeat the six claims in SECURITY.md and broke all six. Three root causes, not nine bugs. (1) classify_destructive FAILED OPEN on any subcommand it could not resolve \u2014 `sf data:delete:bulk` is an oclif alias for the space form, and `sf \"$@\"` / `sf data $V` hide the verb behind an expansion; each returned None and ran a bulk delete with no token and no shield. Colon IDs are normalized now, and an unreadable verb classifies as 'opaque-write' rather than as nothing. (2) The path guards inspected only LITERAL tokens, so `find ~ -name secret -path '*torque*' -exec cat {} +` read the signing secret and the same shape with cp overwrote a gate file \u2014 no token ever showed the path. The rule is now whole-command and root-scoped, so scoped work like `find ./build -exec rm {} +` still runs. (3) split_segments fragments $(...) at the parens, so `cp /tmp/x $(echo hooks)/lib.py` was never one token \u2014 LIVE-demonstrated overwrite. Plus a case-insensitive sObject shield and a glued -XPOST. The allow cases exist because a gate that blocks ordinary work gets switched off. Late addition: the brace guards recursed on ANY token containing { and , \u2014 when _brace_expand could not expand it (a dict literal, a JSON blob, an awk program) it returned the token unchanged and the guard called itself forever. RecursionError crashed the gate, which fails closed, so ordinary commands were denied. These three allow-cases pin the no-progress guard.",
